# Day 6–7 – Authentication & Role-Based Access
## AWS EC2 Self-Service Provisioning Portal

---

## 1. What We Built

### Day 6 — Registration & Login

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `app/schemas.py` | Pydantic request/response models: `UserCreate`, `UserLogin`, `UserOut`, `Token` |
| `app/auth.py` | Password hashing (via `bcrypt` directly) and JWT token creation/verification |
| `app/dependencies.py` | `get_current_user` — decodes the JWT from a request and looks up the real user |
| `app/routes_auth.py` | Three endpoints: `POST /auth/register`, `POST /auth/login`, `GET /auth/me` |

**How it works:**
- On registration, the password is hashed with bcrypt (never stored in plain text) and a new `User` row is created with `role = "user"` by default
- On login, the submitted password is checked against the stored hash; if correct, a JWT access token is issued containing the user's ID and role
- `/auth/me` is a protected route — it requires a valid token and returns the profile of whoever the token belongs to, proving the whole auth chain works

### Day 7 — Role Enforcement

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `app/dependencies.py` (extended) | Added `require_admin` — builds on `get_current_user`, additionally checks the user's role is `admin` |
| `app/routes_auth.py` (extended) | Added `GET /auth/admin-only` — a demo route only admins can successfully call |
| `make_admin.py` | Standalone script to manually promote a user to admin by email |

**Key design decision:** Admin status is **never** self-assignable through the API — nobody can register as admin. Promotion happens only via `make_admin.py`, run directly against the database. This matches real-world practice: admin access should always be a deliberate, out-of-band action, not something a user can grant themselves.

`require_admin` checks the role stored in the **database** on every request, not just what's embedded in the token — so if an admin's role were ever revoked, their existing token would immediately stop granting admin access, rather than staying valid until it expires.

